# Identical to packaging/aur/cordial-git/cordial-git.install. Said here as
# well as in the pkgdesc, because a package registry is where a false promise
# costs most: someone installs, launches, gets a dialog they did not expect,
# and files a bug against a maintainer rather than reading a README.

_note() {
  cat <<'MSG'

Cordial ships no Roblox build, and it cannot download one.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the client comes from Google Play. Cordial
runs the copy Sober has already unpacked, or an APK you point it at yourself:

    ~/.var/app/org.vinegarhq.Sober/data/sober/packages/x86_64/com.roblox.client/

Without one, Cordial starts and tells you it found no Roblox build. That is the
whole of what it can do until you supply one.

Start it with `cordial-shell`, or from your application list. `cordial-run` is
the loader the shell launches and is not what you run by hand.

Cordial is early: you can sign in, load an experience and play with keyboard and
mouse. Text fields do not draw what you type, and the pointer is not captured in
first person. https://github.com/luohoa97/cordial#readme says which claims were
measured and how.

MSG
}

post_install() {
  _note
}

post_upgrade() {
  :
}
